        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 4: PowerShell Variables and Data Structures\nP\u00e1ginas: 100-131\n\u2022 Understanding PowerShell variables  (100)\n  \u2022 Variables are not their contents  (101)\n  \u2022 Naming variables  (103)\n  \u2022 The three common types of PowerShell variable  (104)\n\u2022 Exploring object types  (106)\n  \u2022 What is an object? \u2013 redux  (107)\n\u2022 Discovering value types  (109)\n  \u2022 Booleans  (109)\n  \u2022 Integers  (110)\n  \u2022 Real numbers  (110)\n  \u2022 Char  (111)\n\u2022 Typing explained  (111)\n  \u2022 Dynamic versus static typing  (111)\n  \u2022 Casting variables  (112)\n\u2022 Navigating reference types  (114)\n  \u2022 Arrays  (115)\n  \u2022 Strings  (121)\n  \u2022 Hashtables  (125)\n\u2022 Splatting \u2013 a cool use for hashtables  (129)\n\u2022 Summary  (129)\n\u2022 Exercises  (130)\n\u2022 Further reading  (130)\n4\nPowerShell Variables\nand Data Structures\nIt\u2019s time to really understand what we mean when we talk about variables. Variables are a common\nconcept in computer science and mathematics, so it\u2019s important to have a good grasp of what they\nare and how they are used in PowerShell.\nWe\u2019ll start by exploring what a variable is, both literally and metaphorically. We will look at how we\ncan use them in PowerShell and contrast the way PowerShell works with other languages. We will\nexplore the concept of primitives, the basic building blocks of data, before moving on to the common\ndata structures that PowerShell uses. Finally, we\u2019ll have some fun with splatting, an important and\nuseful technique for simplifying cmdlets.\nIn this chapter, we\u2019re going to cover the following main topics.\n\u2022I Understanding PowerShell variables\n\u2022I Exploring object types\n\u2022I Discovering value types\n\u2022I Typing explained\n\u2022I Navigating reference types\n\u2022I Splatting \u2013 using hashtables for fun and profit",
